    "question": {
      ":question/id": "2befe1a8-7f5c-53ce-a900-1c772ddc5c27",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y={4}^{-4x-2}$ at the point where $x=-1.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={4}^{u},\\,u=-4x-2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}={4}^{u}\\ln(4),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\\\ & ={4}^{u}\\ln(4)\\cdot (-4) \\\\ & =(-4)\\cdot {4}^{u}\\ln(4) \\\\ & =-4\\cdot {4}^{-4x-2}\\ln(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=-1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big|_{x=-1} & =(-4)\\cdot {4}^{-4(-1)-2}\\ln(4) \\\\ & =-4\\cdot {4}^{2}\\ln(4) \\\\ & =- 64 \\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $x=-1$ is $- 64 \\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}.$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\ny_1={4}^{2}=16.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1&=m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(16)&=(- 64 \\ln{\\left(4 \\right)})\\left(x-(-1)\\right) \\\\ y&=- 128 x \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} - 128 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} + 16.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
