      ":question/id": "7c8d97f9-9839-5020-9794-755c5645e6e1",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y={4}^{-x-1}$ at the point where $x=-1.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={4}^{u},\\,u=-x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}={4}^{u}\\ln(4),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\\\ & ={4}^{u}\\ln(4)\\cdot (-1) \\\\ & =(-1)\\cdot {4}^{u}\\ln(4) \\\\ & =-1\\cdot {4}^{-x-1}\\ln(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=-1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big|_{x=-1} & =(-1)\\cdot {4}^{-(-1)-1}\\ln(4) \\\\ & =-1\\cdot {4}^{0}\\ln(4) \\\\ & =- \\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $x=-1$ is $- \\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}.$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\ny_1={4}^{0}=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1&=m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(1)&=(- \\ln{\\left(4 \\right)})\\left(x-(-1)\\right) \\\\ y&=- 2 x \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} - 2 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} + 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
